<github-data-json>
{
  "title": "Defects D0a: durable, classified failure observations for a run's gates",
  "body": "## Problem\nPart of M6.5, Epic D (#823), slice D0a.\n\nEpic D's detectors and routing table need each failure classified where it happened, while the evidence is fresh: machine setup, undeclared requirement, confinement, load, flaky, regression and platform. Today the inputs are scattered, transient or ambiguous.\n\n- **Verdicts are computed on comparisons, not on executions.**\n  - `compareGates` (`src/gates/run.ts:489-546`) synthesizes `not_run` comparisons for checks that never executed when setup failed (`run.ts:491-511`), and treats `still_failing` as non-blocking (`run.ts:531-540`).\n  - A check that timed out and passed on the gates stage's re-run keeps only `firstAttempt` and compares as `pass` (`src/pipeline/engine.ts:1642-1673`, `run.ts:528`).\n  - `retryRegressions` retries only some regressions: never one whose output names a changed file, a timeout, or a configuration mismatch (`run.ts:595-605`).\n- **Nothing durable records an execution's failure.**\n  - The round's comparison reaches disk only as an artifact (`gates-\u003cround\u003e.json`, `engine.ts:1751`), written before the stage's completion checkpoint (`src/pipeline/context.ts:583-590`).\n  - The execution handle is cleared once the result returns (`src/pipeline/gate-execution.ts:130-141`).\n  - So a crash in between re-runs the stage, and if the re-run passes, the first failure leaves no record. A cached stage skips its callback entirely (`context.ts:561-567`).\n- **Identity and attribution aren't durable.**\n  - A resumed stage gets a new stage row (`context.ts:575`). The durable handle records the stage name, round and purpose, not the row id (`gate-execution.ts:122`).\n  - A stored local handle re-executes under the same id after a restart (`src/gates/executor.ts:143-157`).\n  - A LAN job can finish locally (`src/gates/lan.ts:132-157`); `postMergeWorker` (`src/pipeline/post-merge-confirmation.ts:17-22`) already refuses to attribute a fallback to the worker.\n  - Worker OS lives only in heartbeat capabilities that each heartbeat overwrites (`src/db/workers.ts:315-324`), and `GateRun` carries no OS (`run.ts:52-62`).\n  - Host load is read only inside `Scheduler` (`src/scheduler.ts:117-119`).\n\n## Invariant\nFor every run gates stage, from this change on:\n- **What is observed.** Each check or setup step that executed and failed in the stage's primary execution (purpose `initial`), including timeouts the stage later recovered from, has exactly one observation row.\n- **The key.** The row is keyed by the execution's durable identity (handle kind and id) plus phase and name.\n- **Durability.**\n  - The row is written in the engine's first durable step after the result returns, and before the handle is cleared.\n  - A LAN or in-place result that hadn't been recorded is replayed and recorded before anything re-executes.\n  - The only execution with no row is one whose result never reached that step, which is the restart rule's \"interrupted before its checkpoint\", like a daemon killed while the gate ran.\n- **Finalization.**\n  - Every row is finalized exactly once with one class, in the same transaction as its stage's completion checkpoint, or by the run's terminal handling if the stage never completes.\n  - A later stage completion overrides a terminal interim class.\n- **Attribution.** OS and location are those of the execution itself, captured when it ran, never re-read from later state.\n- **Exclusions.**\n  - Synthetic non-executions (`not_run`, docs-only `skipped`) and reused agent-gate results (#798) are never observed.\n  - A passing execution records nothing.\n\n## Requirements\n1. **The evidence checkpoint.**\n   - **When:** in `runGateExecution` (`gate-execution.ts`), when stage is `gates` and purpose is `initial`, insert one row per failed setup step or check of the returned `GateRun`, in one `store.transaction`. This happens immediately after `executeGate` returns, before the `finally` that clears the handle (`gate-execution.ts:130-141`). \"Failed\" means `!ok`, `timedOut`, or `confinementFailed`.\n   - **Key:** `(run id, handle kind, handle id, phase, name)`, with `ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING`. The stage row id is stored for attribution only.\n   - **Replay:**\n     - A kept LAN or in-place handle (kept on shutdown, or a crash before clearing) is re-attached on resume by the existing `resumeGateExecution` path, and its stored result replays through the same insert, as a no-op when the row exists.\n     - That replay happens before any re-execution, because re-attach precedes re-execution in that path.\n   - **Local handles:** `resumeGateExecution` no longer re-executes under a stored `local` handle id. A local execution never survives the daemon, so resume starts a fresh local execution under a new id, and distinct executions never share a key.\n2. **The observation's fields,** normalized at write time and never recomputed later:\n   - phase (`setup` or `check`), name, the redacted command;\n   - run id, stage row id (attribution), round, SHA, handle kind and id;\n   - location:\n     - `coordinator` for local, in-place or fallen-back LAN executions;\n     - the worker name for a LAN job that completed remotely (`executionLocation(ctx, handle)`, generalized from `postMergeWorker`);\n     - `null` when it can't be established;\n   - `os`:\n     - coordinator executions: `process.platform`;\n     - LAN jobs: the job's `worker_os`, a new nullable `gate_jobs` column written in the lease transaction (`workers.ts:372`) from the leasing worker's capabilities at that moment;\n     - legacy jobs with no `worker_os`: `null`, meaning unknown;\n   - `load_per_core`: the coordinator's load, for coordinator executions only, through an injectable `loadSource` in `EngineDeps` that defaults to the scheduler's formula;\n   - `observed_at`;\n   - the failed attempt: exit code, `timedOut`, `confinement`, duration, `failedFiles`, and the output tail after `redactGateOutput`, truncated after redaction;\n   - the signature (requirement 5);\n   - `setup_eligible`: phase `setup` or exit code 127, and not confinement. It is fixed at write time.\n3. **Finalization committed with stage completion.**\n   - `runStage`'s completion checkpoint (`context.ts:583-590`) gains an optional `onComplete` callback. The gates stage passes one that finalizes, from the final comparison:\n     - every observation of this run, round and SHA, whatever its stage row (so crash re-runs are included);\n     - and only those not finalized by an earlier completion.\n   - The callback runs in the same `store.transaction` as the `completedChecks` state write. Either both commit or neither does: a crash before the commit leaves the rows unfinalized for the resumed stage's completion, and after the commit the cached stage skip (`context.ts:561-567`) needs nothing more.\n   - Each row gets `class` and `history`. History is one of `no_retry`, `retry_failed`, `retry_passed`, `retry_rejected` (#893 `retryProvenanceRejected`), `rerun_passed` or `rerun_failed` (the stage's timeout re-run). A row from an earlier stage execution whose check finally passed gets `retry_passed`.\n4. **Classes.** A pure function, `classifyObservation(observation, finalComparison)`, in a new `src/gates/failure-class.ts`. The first matching row wins.\n\n   | # | Observation and history | Class |\n   |---|---|---|\n   | 1 | Confinement on the observed attempt, or the final comparison is `confinement_error` | `confinement` |\n   | 2 | `setup_eligible` | `setup` (requirement 6 may promote it) |\n   | 3 | Observed attempt timed out, then `rerun_passed` or `retry_passed` | `load` |\n   | 4 | Observed attempt timed out, then `rerun_failed`, or no passing attempt | `unclassified`. The load figure stays as evidence only, because the coordinator's load says nothing about a worker. |\n   | 5 | `retry_passed` (provenance valid) | `flaky` |\n   | 6 | `retry_rejected` | from the comparison's pre-retry verdict (`retryOriginal`): `regression` for `regressed` or `targeted_failure` |\n   | 7 | Final verdict `regressed` or `targeted_failure`, history `no_retry` or `retry_failed` | `regression` |\n   | 8 | Final verdict `still_failing` | `base_failure`: the base fails the same check. It is recorded but isn't a defect of the change. D1 excludes it from change-attributed detectors, and D1b may use it as corroboration. |\n   | 9 | `new_failure` (no base evidence), history `no_retry` or `retry_failed` | `unclassified` |\n   | 10 | Anything else | `unclassified` |\n\n5. **The signature** is the `signature` string field of `ciSignature(name, outputTail, command)` (`src/integrations/ci-classifier.ts:76`), with today's semantics unchanged. It is computed from the observed failed attempt's redacted output, never from a later passing result.\n\n   Those semantics keep the selected failure line literally, including durations such as Bun's `[0.10ms]` (`test/ci-classifier.test.ts:702-703`), so the same failure with different timing text gets different signatures. Normalizing that is out of scope.\n6. **Order-independent promotion.**\n   - **The predicate.** A `setup` row is `undeclared_requirement` exactly when another `setup_eligible` row, in any finalization state, exists with:\n     - the same repository and signature;\n     - a different, non-null location;\n     - `observed_at` within 7 days of it, in either direction.\n   - **Evaluation.** Every insert and every finalization of a `setup_eligible` row evaluates the predicate for that row and for each row it matches. Each matching finalized `setup` row is updated to `undeclared_requirement` in the same `BEGIN IMMEDIATE` transaction. The predicate is symmetric and doesn't depend on finalization state, so the result is the same in every insertion and finalization order.\n   - **Null locations** never match and are never promoted.\n7. **Terminal handling.**\n   - When a run reaches a terminal status, each unfinalized observation of the run is finalized from the observed attempt alone (rows 1, 2 and 4), with history `interrupted`.\n   - A later completion of the same stage in a resumed run overrides that class (requirement 3).\n8. **Persistence.**\n   - A new migration, `src/db/migrations/\u003cUTC\u003e-failure-observations.sql`, creates `failure_observations`:\n     - the requirement 2 fields;\n     - `class` and `history`, nullable until finalized, with no CHECK constraint;\n     - `finalized_at` and `interim` (set by terminal handling);\n     - the unique key;\n     - an index on `(repo, signature, observed_at)`.\n   - The same migration adds `gate_jobs.worker_os TEXT`.\n   - Inserts use explicit column lists only. `gate_jobs` inserts already name their columns (`workers.ts:351`).\n   - The read path for D1 is `Store.failureObservations({ repo?, since?, class?, finalized? })`.\n9. **Privacy.** The table stays local. Worker names can be machine names, so a code comment on `location` says D1a must map it to a placeholder before anything is published.\n\n## Tests\nEach must fail without its change. Integration tests use the real gates stage with the fake harness and fake gate runners, and restart with a real engine resume, as the existing restart tests do.\n\n**Durability and identity (adversarial)**\n1. **Kept in-place handle.** An in-place job's result is recorded, then the daemon shuts down before the handle is cleared (the shutdown keep path, `gateShutdown`). A real engine restart re-attaches, the result replays, and exactly one row exists, keyed by the job id.\n2. **Fault before the evidence checkpoint.** At a new fault point between `executeGate` returning and the insert:\n   - **LAN or in-place:** the resume replays and records the result once, before any re-execution.\n   - **Local:** the handle remains; the resume starts a new local execution under a new id; that execution's failure is recorded under the new key, and no row shares a key across the two.\n3. **Crash after the checkpoint, before stage completion.** Both executions' rows are finalized exactly once, by the resumed stage's completion transaction:\n   - the first execution's row is `flaky` (`retry_passed`) if the re-run passes;\n   - and `regression` if it fails.\n4. **Crash after the completion transaction:** the cached stage is skipped, and no row is finalized again.\n\n**Eligibility**\n\n5. A timeout that passes on the stage's re-run is observed and classed `load`, though its comparison is `pass`.\n6. A setup failure observes the setup step only. The synthetic `not_run` comparisons create no rows.\n7. A `still_failing` check is classed `base_failure`.\n8. A docs-only skipped check, and a reused agent-gate result, create no rows.\n\n**Histories, through the real stage**\n\n9. An unretried regression whose output names a changed file is `regression`/`no_retry`.\n10. A regression with a failed retry is `regression`; with a passing retry, `flaky`; with a retry that changed tracked files (`retry_rejected`), `regression`.\n11. Confinement beats every other rule, including a timeout.\n\n**Attribution**\n\n12. A LAN job that fell back to local is located at `coordinator`, with the coordinator's OS.\n13. **OS capture.**\n    - A LAN job's `worker_os` is captured at lease time, and the worker's heartbeat reporting another OS afterwards doesn't change the observation.\n    - A legacy job row with no `worker_os` yields `os = null`.\n14. `load_per_core` comes from the injected `loadSource`.\n\n**Promotion (both orders)**\n\n15. Setup rows A (location X) and B (location Y) share a signature, 2 days apart:\n    - A finalized before B is observed;\n    - B finalized while A is still unfinalized;\n    - A and B finalizing concurrently on two connections.\n\n    In every case, both end `undeclared_requirement`. Eight days apart, or with one null location, neither is promoted.\n\n**Signature, privacy and terminal**\n\n16. The signature equals `ciSignature(...).signature` of the failed attempt, also when the final result is a passing retry.\n17. A registered credential in the output appears in neither the evidence nor the signature.\n18. A run cancelled mid-stage finalizes its observations with history `interrupted`. A resumed run that completes that stage replaces the class.\n\n## Compatibility\n- **Rollback:**\n  - The migration adds a table, an index and a nullable column on `gate_jobs`.\n  - The previous release never reads `failure_observations`. Its `gate_jobs` inserts name their columns, so the new column doesn't break them.\n  - Check the migration with Homebrew's SQLite (`Database.setCustomSQLite`), as AGENTS.md asks.\n- **Restart and resume:**\n  - Observations are durable before the handle is cleared, and idempotent by execution identity.\n  - Finalization commits with the stage's completion checkpoint.\n  - A re-execution only ever adds rows for a real new execution.\n  - There are no external side effects.\n- **Runs created before this change** have no rows. D1 treats the absence as no observation.\n- **Polling only:** no involvement.\n- **Hot path:** one transaction per failing execution and one per completed stage. Nothing runs per output line.\n\n## Out of scope\n- Land checks and post-merge gates (D0b). D1 consumers wait for D0b.\n- Baseline gates (they describe the base) and agent-gate executions (#798).\n- Filing issues and publishing machine names (D1a).\n- Detectors and flake rates (D1b–D1d). Pass counts aren't recorded.\n- Signature normalization.\n- Changing any verdict, retry or blocking decision. D0a only records.\n\n## Budget\nAbout 260 changed source lines plus tests:\n- the evidence checkpoint and the local-handle change ~45;\n- location, OS and load capture, plus `worker_os` at lease ~30;\n- migration and store ~45;\n- the classifier and histories ~70;\n- finalization committed with completion, terminal handling and promotion ~60;\n- a fault point ~10.\n\nStop at 320 and report.\n\n---\n\n"
}
